Good news: I do have auth/jwt.py here, so I can explain your exact implementation directly.

Access token validation (validate):
- decode step:
  - pyjwt.decode(token, SECRET, algorithms=[ALGO])
  - This verifies:
    - signature (HS256 with shared SECRET)
    - exp claim (expiry) automatically
- error handling:
  - ExpiredSignatureError -> returns None
  - InvalidTokenError -> returns None (bad signature, malformed token, wrong alg, etc.)
- claim-type guard:
  - if claims["typ"] == "refresh" -> returns None
  - prevents using a refresh token as an access token
- success path:
  - returns claims dict

Refresh flow (refresh):
- decode refresh token with same SECRET/ALGO
- on any InvalidTokenError -> None
  - includes expired refresh tokens too
- explicit type check:
  - typ must equal "refresh", otherwise None
- success path:
  - returns issue(claims["sub"])
  - i.e., new access + new refresh token pair is minted

Token issuance (issue):
- access token payload: {"sub","iat","exp"} with 15 min TTL
- refresh token payload: {"sub","iat","exp","typ":"refresh"} with 7 day TTL
- both signed with same key and algorithm

Key edge cases / security implications in this implementation:
- No revocation store:
  - stolen refresh token remains usable until expiry
- No refresh token rotation enforcement:
  - it does mint a new refresh token, but does not invalidate old one server-side
- Shared symmetric secret for all tokens:
  - if SECRET leaks, all tokens are forgeable
- Hardcoded dev secret:
  - SECRET = "dev-secret-change-me" is unsafe outside local fixture use
- Minimal claim validation:
  - no issuer (iss), audience (aud), jti, device/session binding, or nonce checks
- Null-only error signaling:
  - validate/refresh both return None for all failures, so callers can’t distinguish expired vs malformed vs tampered without extra logging

In short:
- For a benchmark fixture, this is clean and intentionally minimal.
- For production, the biggest gap is refresh-token lifecycle control (rotation + revocation persistence), then secret management and stronger claim policy.
